= Part 1: History of Diphtheria

== Outline Gap Answers

#v(0.2em)

I.A.1: First described by *Hippocrates*

I.A.2: Greek name means *leather*

I.B.1: Scientists developed *serum* therapy

I.B.2: Won first Nobel Prize in *1901*

I.C.1: A *safer* vaccine was made

#v(0.3em)

== Comprehension Question Answers

#v(0.2em)

1. The Greek physician *Hippocrates* first described diphtheria in the 4th or 5th century BCE. He is known as the father of medicine.

2. Kitasato and von Behring developed *serum therapy*. They injected animals with diphtheria toxins to produce antibodies, then gave those antibodies to other animals and eventually humans to stop them from getting sick.

3. A safer vaccine was made by a *French researcher* in *1923*.

#pagebreak()

= Part 2: Vaccines and Outbreak

== Outline Gap Answers

#v(0.2em)

II.A.1: *93%* of 5-year-olds are vaccinated

II.A.2: A *booster* is given at ages 11-13

II.B.1: More than *230* cases

II.B.2: Many cases in *Indigenous* communities

#v(0.3em)

== Comprehension Question Answers

#v(0.2em)

1. *93%* of Australian 5-year-olds have had all their recommended doses of the diphtheria vaccine.

2. A *booster shot* is available for 11-to-13-year-olds through school programs.

3. Most cases have been found in *Queensland, Western Australia, South Australia, and the Northern Territory*. Many of those affected are *adults living in Indigenous communities*.

#pagebreak()

= Part 3: Government Response

== Outline Gap Answers

#v(0.2em)

III.A.1: Over *100* Aboriginal languages

III.A.2: Need to fight *misinformation*

III.B.1: Over *10,000* vaccinated in the NT

III.B.2: New cases are going *down*

#v(0.3em)

== Comprehension Question Answers

#v(0.2em)

1. Minister McCarthy said there are over 100 Aboriginal languages and the government needs to make sure there is *good communication, not misinformation*, getting to remote and regional communities.

2. Minister Butler said diseases like diphtheria and measles that were thought to be in the past are showing a *re-emergence* because childhood immunisation rates are dropping in many countries.

3. The outbreak isn't over yet, but more than *10,000 people* in the NT have recently been vaccinated and the number of new cases has gone *down*.

#pagebreak()

= Part 4: Discussion — Model Answers

== Think-Pair-Share Question 1 (Controlled — with scaffolding)

*Should vaccination be required for everyone? Use at least one piece of evidence from the video.*

*Model answer using the suggested structure:*

I think *vaccination should be compulsory* because the video showed that *before vaccines, thousands of children died from diphtheria*. When the vaccine became widespread, cases fell a lot. The video also showed that *in parts of Australia, 93% of 5-year-olds are vaccinated*, but in communities with lower rates, there was an outbreak. This means that *when fewer people get vaccinated, diseases can come back — so everyone needs to be protected*.

*Alternative model answer:*

I think *vaccination should be compulsory for most people, but there should be exceptions for medical reasons*. The video showed that *more than 10,000 people in the NT got vaccinated recently to stop the outbreak*. This means that *vaccines work, but some people may have health problems that make vaccination unsafe for them*.

== Think-Pair-Share Question 2 (Free practice — no scaffolding)

*How can governments make sure everyone has access to vaccines, especially in remote communities?*

*Model response:*

The video mentioned that there are over 100 Aboriginal languages and that some communities face barriers to accessing vaccines. Governments can send mobile health teams to remote areas and provide information in local languages. They can also work with community leaders to build trust, since the video showed that misinformation is a problem. Minister McCarthy said the right information needs to get out to remote communities, so translation and local health workers are important. Governments should also make vaccines free and easy to get, just like the NT outbreak response where 10,000 people were vaccinated in a few weeks.
